! conclusion: Reproducibility as a Teaching Habit

  + context: You want researchers to work reproducibly, and you are trying to decide when to tell them.

  + IF:
    Your discipline depends on shared code and data, and you teach students who will shortly be producing analyses of their own.

  + HOWEVER:
    Reproducibility is usually introduced as a compliance topic to people who already have working habits, which makes it retrofitting: a tidy-up performed at submission on an analysis that was never built to be inspected. By then the cost is real and the benefit is invisible.

  + THEN:
    Teach it as the ordinary way to do the work, at the point where habits form. Literate programming is the concrete vehicle — analyses written so that code, data and narrative travel together — so that a reproducible document is simply what an analysis looks like, not an extra artefact produced afterwards. Then release the teaching materials themselves openly and under a permissive licence, so the course models the practice it teaches and other educators can adapt it.

  + BECAUSE:
    A habit formed first is cheaper than a habit corrected later, and it transfers: students carry it into projects, dissertations, and jobs. Publishing the course openly also converts local teaching effort into a reusable resource, and makes the department's claim about reproducibility checkable by anyone.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Identify the earliest module where students write their first analysis, and start there rather than at doctoral level.]
    next[Deposit the materials with a DOI and a permissive licence so reuse is citable — see or/snapshot-with-story-doi.]
    next[For an example in genomics and data analysis teaching, contact the Literate Programming in R team in the School of Medical and Biological Sciences.]
